{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Torn off in her mouth.</b> Some say it is a doubled word in meaning, like אדמת עפר (Daniel 12:2), and they interpret טָרָף like 'an olive leaf,' and likewise כל טרפי צמחה (Ezekiel 17:9). The proof that under the resh is a kamatz gadol like זָהָב and עָשָׁן. What seems correct to me is that the bet is supplementary, like the bet in 'ברוחו שמים שפרה' (Job 26:13), and it appears with one [syllable] (Job 23:13), and it comes with a kamatz: 'כי הוא טָרָף' (Hosea 6:1), אריה שָׁאַג (Amos 3:8) and their companions. Or טָרָף is kamatz because it is an adjective, like חָכָם, and the meaning is like טרוף, for there is an adjective resembling the [active] participle and there is an adjective resembling the [passive] participle."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:43:47.086397488Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}